.Dd November 7, 2026
.Dt XATTR 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xattr
.Nd list, show, set and remove extended attributes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl lrsvx
.Ar
.Nm
.Fl p
.Op Fl lrsvx
.Ar name
.Ar
.Nm
.Fl w
.Op Fl rsx
.Ar name value
.Ar
.Nm
.Fl d
.Op Fl rs
.Ar name
.Ar
.Nm
.Fl c
.Op Fl rs
.Ar
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
works on the extended attributes of each
.Ar file .
With no mode option, it lists the names of the attributes.
.Pp
The modes are:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl p
Print the value of the attribute
.Ar name .
.It Fl w
Set the attribute
.Ar name
to
.Ar value .
.It Fl d
Remove the attribute
.Ar name .
.It Fl c
Remove every attribute.
.El
.Pp
The other options are:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl h , Fl Fl help
Print the usage and exit.
.It Fl l
Print each attribute's name and value together.
A hex value is printed with offsets and its characters.
.It Fl r
Descend into directories.
.It Fl s
Act on a symbolic link, not on the file it points to.
.It Fl v
Print the file name before each result.
This is the default with
.Fl r
or with more than one file.
.It Fl x
Read and print values as hex.
.El
.Pp
.Fl l
can't be used with
.Fl w
or
.Fl d .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 if every file was handled, and 1 if any failed.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Set an attribute and print it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ xattr -w org.example.note hello file.txt
$ xattr -p org.example.note file.txt
hello
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr getxattr 2 ,
.Xr listxattr 2 ,
.Xr removexattr 2 ,
.Xr setxattr 2
